    "body": "@smaug---- So the goal of the changes that I'm reverting was to ensure that if the `dir` attribute was used in the shadow tree on the *ancestor* of a `slot` element, that `dir` attribute would have no effects on the CSS directionality of the slotted content, and keep the HTML directionality (which inherits down the node tree) in sync with the CSS directionality (which inherits down the flattened tree).  (Note that this was not done when `dir` was used on the `slot` element itself.)  These changes worked by explicitly specifying CSS `direction` on all `slot` elements, based on their HTML directionality, which inherits from the shadow host.\r\n\r\nThe result of reverting this is basically that:\r\n* it's a bad idea to use the `dir` attribute on a part of a shadow tree structure that has `slot` element descendants (because it would break inheritance of CSS direction through the shadow tree back into the slotted light DOM)\r\n\r\nwhich is in addition to the existing:\r\n* it's a bad idea to use `dir` other than `auto` on a `slot` element\r\n\r\n(Perhaps I should mention both of these in authoring advice in the spec.)\r\n\r\nThe reason to revert these is that the mechanism of these changes breaks cases where the author uses CSS `direction` rather than HTML `dir` (which means the author has made the two un-synchronized) by resynchronizing them for any descendants of a `slot` element.  This means that the change breaks author-specified CSS `direction` from inheriting through any element that uses Shadow DOM.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
